You upload your code files as flat attachments - there are no folders once they're uploaded. So: give each file a name that shows where it used to live, using _ in place of /. For example, a file that was at app/agent/workflow.py gets uploaded as app_agent_workflow.py. This keeps file names unique and lets a reviewer picture your project's structure even without real folders.

You also don't need a separate manifest and rationale document - one file covering both, like this one, is enough.

Where things are

Agentic loop/orchestration: app_agent_workflow.py

Tool definitions: app_agent_tools.py check_room_availability(), lookup_room_catalog(), confirm_booking()

-text room matching: app_agent_parser.py match_room_name(), turns a phrase like "the small room on 3" into a catalog room ID

Free

Persistence/database code: app_models_database.py (connection + queries), schema in app_models_models.py

Rationale

I built room-lookup and availability-checking as two separate tools instead of one. A requester's room name (like "the small room on 3") often doesn't match the catalog's room ID directly, and that step can fail on its own - separate from whether the room is actually free. Keeping them apart made it easier to tell "couldn't find the room" apart from "found it, but it's busy."

The brief didn't say what to do if a requester books a slot that's already tentatively (not yet confirmed) held by someone else. I decided a tentative hold shouldn't block a new request outright, but the new request should need manual confirmation instead of auto-approving. Someone could reasonably argue a tentative hold should block the slot completely - I picked the softer option because nothing in the brief said holds were final.

Before saving a room that the Al picked from a free-text request, I check that the room ID it returned actually exists in the catalog. If it doesn't, I don't save it - I send it for manual review instead of guessing.

One thing I left out: there's no way to cancel or edit a booking once it's made only new bookings are handled. With more time, that's what I'd add next.

